The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (snet->hook_ref4 == 0) {
    err = nf_register_net_hooks(net, ipv4_synproxy_ops, ARRAY_SIZE(ipv4_synproxy_ops));
    if (err) return err;
}
snet->hook_ref4++;
```
```c
if (snet->hook_ref6 == 0) {
    err = nf_register_net_hooks(net, ipv6_synproxy_ops, ARRAY_SIZE(ipv6_synproxy_ops));
    if (err) return err;
}
snet->hook_ref6++;
```
Reason:
Without a mutex, two concurrent calls to `nf_synproxy_ipv4_init` or `nf_synproxy_ipv6_init` can both read `hook_ref4` (or `hook_ref6`) as zero and then both attempt to register the same hooks, leading to double registration. Similarly, concurrent `_fini` calls can decrement the reference counter incorrectly, causing premature unregistration or a use-after-free when the hooks are still in use. The lack of serialization on the reference count and hook registration/unregistration creates a race condition.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
static DEFINE_MUTEX(synproxy_mutex);
```
```c
mutex_lock(&synproxy_mutex);
if (snet->hook_ref4 == 0) {
    err = nf_register_net_hooks(net, ipv4_synproxy_ops, ARRAY_SIZE(ipv4_synproxy_ops));
    if (err) goto out;
}
snet->hook_ref4++;
out:
mutex_unlock(&synproxy_mutex);
```
```c
mutex_lock(&synproxy_mutex);
snet->hook_ref4--;
if (snet->hook_ref4 == 0)
    nf_unregister_net_hooks(net, ipv4_synproxy_ops, ARRAY_SIZE(ipv4_synproxy_ops));
mutex_unlock(&synproxy_mutex);
```
Reason:
A static mutex `synproxy_mutex` is introduced and acquired before any access to `hook_ref4`/`hook_ref6` and before calling hook registration or unregistration. This ensures that only one thread can execute the critical section (check reference count, register/unregister hooks, update the count) at a time. The error path now jumps to `out` where the mutex is released, preventing a deadlock. The same pattern is applied to IPv6 and fini functions, guaranteeing atomicity of the refcount and hook lifecycle operations, thus eliminating the race condition.

